# Lints this gate does not refuse, one per line, as the lint name followed by the
# reason it is not refused. A line carrying a name and no reason is refused by
# .github/lint/lint.sh, because a bare identifier is a lint somebody turned off
# and nobody argued with.
#
# Every run prints this file with its reasons, so an exclusion is read at the
# moment somebody reads a verdict rather than found later by somebody auditing.
#
# A line here is a debt rather than a dispensation. Each reason says what would
# retire it.
#
# THIS REGISTER IS EMPTY OF ENTRIES TODAY, AND THAT IS A FACT ABOUT THE TREE
# RATHER THAN ABOUT THE RULE. There is almost no code here yet, so nothing has
# had a chance to need an exclusion. The refusal above is proven by the fixtures
# in .github/lint/lint.sh rather than by anything in this file, which is the only
# way a rule with no live subject can be proven at all.
#
# The name is written the way the analyser writes it, `clippy::` and the lint,
# because that is the string the run passes back to the analyser and a second
# spelling would be a second thing to keep in step.
